config VS_VOICE_MAX_TURNS
	int "VelaSight idle assistant maximum turns per conversation"
	default 20
	range 2 100
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Local cap on how many question/answer turns one conversation can
		retain.  Every accepted turn is kept verbatim for the final CHAT
		record even after its replay context has been semantically compacted.

config VS_VOICE_TURN_STORAGE_MAX_BYTES
	int "VelaSight retained full-turn storage budget (bytes)"
	default 131072
	range 16384 1048576
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Hard aggregate heap budget for the complete UTF-8 questions and
		answers retained until CHAT persistence.  The implementation never
		clips a turn to fit this budget: an answer that would exceed it is
		rejected explicitly and all earlier accepted turns are still saved.

config VS_VOICE_CONTEXT_MAX_BYTES
	int "VelaSight idle assistant conversation context budget (bytes)"
	default 65536
	range 8192 1048576
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Upper bound on the JSON-escaped conversation context replayed into
		the next model request.  At the configured percentage, prior turns
		are replaced in the prompt by a semantic summary.  Their complete
		text remains in the separate retained-turn budget above so the final
		CHAT history record is lossless.

config VS_VOICE_CONTEXT_COMPACT_PERCENT
	int "VelaSight idle assistant context compaction threshold (percent)"
	default 80
	range 50 95
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Percentage of CONFIG_VS_VOICE_CONTEXT_MAX_BYTES at which the
		running conversation is compacted (see above).  The product
		requirement this implements is stated in terms of the model's
		own maximum context (mimo-v2.5 advertises a 1M-token window);
		CONFIG_VS_VOICE_CONTEXT_MAX_BYTES is deliberately a much smaller,
		locally-enforced budget, because this device's own conversation
		length in one sitting never approaches a million tokens and a
		budget sized to it would make local RAM the only limit that
		exists in practice.  See vs_voice.c's chat-context module for the
		full reasoning.
config VS_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_RING_BYTES
	int "VelaSight TTS playback staging ring (bytes)"
	default 655360
	range 16384 2097152
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Size of the ring vs_audio.c stages TTS audio in, taken from PSRAM
		with the SRAM heap only as a fallback.

		The DAC pipeline itself is only CONFIG_AUDIO_NUM_BUFFERS x
		CONFIG_AUDIO_BUFFER_NUMBYTES, which is a quarter of a second at
		16 kHz.  Writing the WebSocket's PCM straight into that would pace
		the network at the speed of the speaker.

		The default is 20 seconds of 16 kHz mono audio, which is what the
		PSRAM heap can actually spare: measured on hardware it has about
		900 KiB free at the point playback opens, so asking for the 40
		seconds that would hold the longest possible answer only produced
		a halving retry and a warning on every reply.

		A ring that fills is no longer dangerous, only slow.  Since the
		client answers the server's keep-alive pings, a blocked write
		merely applies backpressure and the reply arrives at the speed it
		is spoken.  What the size still buys is headroom against the
		opposite problem: audio arriving slower than it plays, which is
		what CONFIG_VS_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_PREBUFFER_MS addresses.

config VS_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_PREBUFFER_MS
	int "VelaSight TTS playback pre-buffer (ms)"
	default 1000
	range 0 10000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How much audio to accumulate before the DAC is allowed to start
		consuming it.

		The failure this prevents is the opposite of a full ring.  The TTS
		service produces speech in bursts and slower than real time -- one
		measured reply delivered about four seconds of audio over five and
		a half seconds of wall clock -- so a player that starts on the
		first chunk runs the ring dry between bursts.  The driver handles
		that without complaining: its transmit interrupt masks itself when
		no buffer is queued and re-arms on the next one, so an empty ring
		is silence rather than an error, and the reply comes out audibly
		chopped.

		One second is enough for the arrival rate measured here.  With
		audio arriving at a fraction r of real time, a reply of T seconds
		needs T(1-r)/r of head start to play without a gap; the measured
		r was 0.73 over a 2.2 second reply, which needs 0.8 s.

		It was 3000, and that was worse than useless: a whole reply is
		often smaller than three seconds of audio, so the level never
		reached the mark and only vs_audio_playback_drain() could release
		the gate -- which happens after the TTS stream ends, five seconds
		later than the audio was actually in hand.  A timeout now releases
		the gate as well, so a short reply no longer waits for audio that
		is never coming.

		Zero starts playback on the first chunk.

		This is the starting value and the floor. The actual watermark
		adapts at runtime between this and
		CONFIG_VS_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_PREBUFFER_MAX_MS.

config VS_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_PREBUFFER_MAX_MS
	int "VelaSight TTS playback pre-buffer ceiling (ms)"
	default 4000
	range 0 10000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Upper bound on the adaptive playback watermark.

		The watermark doubles after any reply that starved and decays
		slowly after replies that did not, because the arrival rate is a
		property of the service and the link rather than of one sentence --
		measured on this hardware it swings between 0.60x and 1.93x real
		time, so no single value is right for both.  It is remembered
		across replies; relearning it per sentence would starve the first
		one every time.

		The ceiling exists because the watermark is also the delay before
		a reply starts speaking.  Beyond a few seconds it is better to
		accept a gap than to make the user wait, and a link that cannot
		sustain speech at four seconds of head start has a problem this
		cannot paper over.

config VS_VOICE_TTS_SPEAKER
	string "VelaSight TTS voice (Volcengine voice_type)"
	default "zh_female_meilinvyou_emo_v2_mars_bigtts"
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Volcengine voice_type used for spoken replies, seeded into the
		agent config store next to the ASR cluster.  The product default is
		the female voice zh_female_meilinvyou_emo_v2_mars_bigtts, which is
		what Volcengine's own reference client uses and which pairs with the
		volcano_tts cluster this firmware sends.  Leave empty to fall back
		to packages/ai_agent's compiled-in male voice,
		zh_male_beijingxiaoye_emo_v2_mars_bigtts.

		Stay within the emo_v2_mars_bigtts family: a voice from another
		family such as *_moon_bigtts belongs to a different cluster and the
		request, which sends cluster=volcano_tts, would be refused.

		Voices are entitlements, not just identifiers: an account that has
		not activated the one named here is refused, and the refusal shows
		up as "[volc_tts_ws] server error" in the log rather than as a
		configuration complaint.  Check the console for the voices your
		app_id actually has, and put one of those here.

		Known-good fallback if this voice is not activated:
		zh_male_beijingxiaoye_emo_v2_mars_bigtts

config VS_AUDIO_CAPTURE_SETTLE_BUFFERS
	int "VelaSight capture buffers discarded while the ADC settles"
	default 2
	range 0 32
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Upper bound on how many driver buffers may be dropped at the start
		of a recording because the analog front end is still settling.

		This is a ceiling, not a quota.  Only buffers that are saturated
		almost sample for sample are actually dropped, and the first
		buffer that is not ends the phase, so an utterance keeps its
		opening syllables even when the user starts talking immediately.

		Kept deliberately low.  The second reason this used to exist -- a
		local VAD reading full-scale settling noise as speech -- is gone
		along with the VAD, and the remaining risk runs the other way:
		every buffer dropped here is audio the recognizer never sees, and
		the recognizer handles a short burst of noise far better than it
		handles a missing first word.  At the default 2048-byte buffer this
		bounds the loss at about 128 ms of 16 kHz mono audio.

config VS_VOICE_ASR_CLUSTER
	string "Volcengine streaming ASR cluster"
	default "volcengine_streaming_common"
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Cluster name sent in the streaming ASR request, written into
		ai_agent's config store next to the provisioned app_id and token.

		The service turns this into the entitlement it checks.
		"volcengine_streaming_common" maps to resource id
		volc.streamingasr.common.cn, and an app that has not been granted
		that resource is refused with HTTP 403 and backend_code 45000030
		("requested resource not granted") -- note that this is not an
		authentication failure, so the app_id and token can be perfectly
		valid and the request still be rejected.

		Change this only to match what the Volcengine console shows as
		enabled for the app whose credentials were provisioned.  Switching
		to the large-model streaming ASR service is not just a cluster
		rename: it uses a different endpoint and request protocol than
		AGENT_DOUBAO_ASR_WS_PATH implements.

config VS_AUDIO_MIC_GAIN
	int "VelaSight microphone analog pre-amplifier gain"
	default 6
	range 0 15
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Analog MIC gain sent to the capture driver before it configures the
		ADC (its private BK7258_AUDIOIOC_SET_CAPGAIN ioctl).

		This stage sits ahead of the ADC, so it is the only gain that
		improves the signal-to-noise ratio -- and the only one that can
		clip irrecoverably.  Tune it against the "capture level" line the
		voice worker logs after every utterance, which reports peak and RMS
		on the samples' own 0..32767 scale.

		Measured on this board, all against that full scale:

		  gain 0, empty quiet room ............ rms 28
		  gain 3, several people talking,
		          clearly audible to a person . rms 55, peak 541
		  gain 7, near-field speech ........... rms 2671, peak 32767 (clipped)

		The middle row is the case that matters and the reason the default
		is not lower: conversation a person hears without effort sat barely
		above the empty room's noise floor, and the recognizer answered
		"no valid speech" every time.  Far-field pickup simply needs much
		more gain than near-field does.

		The default trades against the third row.  It is below the setting
		that clipped on close speech while leaving room for the far-field
		case to reach a usable level.  If peak reaches 32767, lower this;
		if the recognizer still finds nothing while peak stays in the low
		thousands, raise it.  Prefer this over the digital gain below.

config VS_AUDIO_ADC_GAIN
	int "VelaSight ADC digital gain"
	default 45
	range 0 63
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Digital gain applied after the ADC, where 45 (0x2d) is 0 dB and the
		range spans roughly -45 dB to +18 dB in 1 dB steps.

		Digital gain cannot improve the signal-to-noise ratio -- it
		amplifies the noise floor with the signal -- so raise
		VS_AUDIO_MIC_GAIN first and use this only for the last few dB once
		the analog stage is as high as it can go without clipping.

config VS_AUDIO_CAPTURE_RING_BYTES
	int "VelaSight capture staging ring (bytes)"
	default 131072
	range 16384 1048576
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Size of the ring a reader thread stages captured audio in, taken
		from PSRAM with the SRAM heap only as a fallback.

		The consumer hands every chunk to the ASR service over TLS, which
		blocks.  The driver holds only CONFIG_AUDIO_NUM_BUFFERS x
		CONFIG_AUDIO_BUFFER_NUMBYTES -- about 256 ms at 16 kHz -- and its
		interrupt handler discards samples once that queue is empty,
		reporting nothing at all.  A network write that stalled longer than
		that therefore lost speech invisibly: a measured 15 s window came
		back holding 12.7 s of audio, and the recognizer saw chopped words.

		This ring is the slack between the two, so a stall costs buffer
		space instead of audio.  The default is four seconds of 16 kHz mono.
		If the "capture level" log line ever reports dropped bytes, the
		consumer fell further behind than this covers.

# Social-session cloud (app/velasight/vs_cloud.c).
#
# These configure the team's own /contest/v1/* server, not the MiMo or
# Volcengine services the idle assistant uses.  Only options vs_cloud.c
# actually reads are listed; the capture cadence and alert debounce options
# from the integration plan belong to vs_social.c and arrive with it.

config VS_SOCIAL_CLOUD_HOST
	string "VelaSight social cloud host"
	default ""
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Hostname or IPv4 address of the social-session cloud, with no
		scheme and no port -- both of those are separate options.

		Empty is a meaningful value, not an oversight: it means nothing
		has been provisioned, and every cloud operation then fails with
		-ENODATA without opening a socket.  Social mode refuses to start
		rather than timing out against a host that was never set.

		A dotted quad skips DNS entirely, which is what makes bring-up
		against the cloud document's own worked examples
		(http://127.0.0.1:18080) work before any resolver is configured.

config VS_SOCIAL_CLOUD_PORT
	int "VelaSight social cloud port"
	default 18080
	range 1 65535
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Defaults to the port used throughout the cloud interface
		document's curl examples.  Set 443 together with
		VS_SOCIAL_CLOUD_TLS for a real deployment.

config VS_SOCIAL_CLOUD_TLS
	bool "Reach the social cloud over TLS"
	default n
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Send /contest/v1/* over HTTPS through packages/ai_agent's
		vela_tls.c instead of cleartext HTTP.

		Off by default because the cloud is not there yet: the interface
		document specifies cleartext for the device-facing endpoints and
		its examples are plain HTTP, and no certificate chain has been
		captured for a real host.

		Understand what turning this on currently does and does not buy.
		vela_tls.c negotiates with MBEDTLS_SSL_VERIFY_OPTIONAL and this
		board has no CA bundle and no trusted time -- it forces the clock
		to a fixed 2026 value when it finds the year unset -- so the
		result is encryption without authentication.  That is strictly
		better than cleartext against a passive observer and no defence
		at all against an active one.  Do not describe a build with this
		enabled as having verified certificates until SNTP, a CA bundle
		and VERIFY_REQUIRED are all in place; see
		docs/plans/BK7258_OPENVELA_MIMO_NETWORK_PORTING_PLAN.md for the
		order those have to land in.

		This setting is independent of the presigned upload URL, whose
		scheme comes from the cloud's own response.

config VS_SOCIAL_DEVICE_ID
	string "VelaSight social cloud device id override"
	default ""
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Normally left empty.  The identifier is derived from the wlan0
		MAC address, which is unique and survives reboots without
		needing to be stored anywhere.

		This is the fallback for a board whose MAC cannot be read, and an
		escape hatch for cloud-side testing that wants a fixed, readable
		name.  Only letters, digits, '-', '_' and '.' are accepted;
		anything else is rejected at startup with a log line.

config VS_SOCIAL_UPLOAD_CONTENT_TYPE
	bool "Send Content-Type when transferring to the presigned URL"
	default y
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Whether the PUT that carries the JPEG or Ogg bytes to the
		presigned URL declares image/jpeg or audio/ogg.

		This exists as an option because it cannot be decided from this
		side.  Object stores compute the presigned signature over a
		specific set of headers, and Content-Type is sometimes one of
		them: if it was signed, omitting it is rejected, and if it was
		not, sending it is rejected.  Both failures come back as HTTP 403
		with an XML body.

		Default is to send it, which is the more common signing scheme.
		If uploads fail with 403 while registration succeeds, turn this
		off before looking anywhere else -- vs_cloud.c names this option
		in that specific log line for exactly that reason.

config VS_SOCIAL_REG_RESP_BYTES
	int "VelaSight social cloud registration response buffer (bytes)"
	default 4096
	range 1024 32768
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Buffer for the small responses: session open, upload
		registration, session close, and the object store's reply to a
		transfer.  The largest thing that lands here is a presigned URL,
		or an object store's XML error document.

		Sized separately from the result buffer below because one of
		these is allocated for every frame and every audio chunk, and
		sizing them all for a full session's timelines would waste PSRAM
		several times a second.

config VS_SOCIAL_RESP_MAX_BYTES
	int "VelaSight social cloud result response buffer (bytes)"
	default 65536
	range 8192 262144
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Buffer for getResult.  The end-of-session response is the large
		one: it carries the text minutes plus a full emotion timeline and
		a full audio timeline, one entry per sampled frame and per
		recognised sentence, so it grows with session length.

		A session whose minutes exceed this is reported as -E2BIG rather
		than truncated, because a clipped JSON record would be persisted
		and then fail to parse afterwards. If that happens, raise this.

config VS_SOCIAL_DOWNLOAD_MAX_BYTES
	int "VelaSight social cloud download budget (bytes)"
	default 262144
	range 16384 2097152
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Upper bound on a body fetched by vs_cloud_download(), which in
		practice means the spoken minutes at ttsMinutes.  The default is
		a few minutes of Ogg Opus at speech bitrates.

config VS_SOCIAL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "VelaSight social cloud connect timeout (ms)"
	default 3000
	range 500 30000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How long a cleartext connect may take before it is abandoned.
		The connect is non-blocking with an explicit poll() so this is
		the real bound, not the stack's own much longer one.

		Keep it short.  A session uploads several times a second, and a
		host that has gone away must not stall the upload thread for
		longer than the queue behind it can absorb.

config VS_SOCIAL_IO_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "VelaSight social cloud send/receive timeout (ms)"
	default 8000
	range 1000 60000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Socket send and receive timeout for the cleartext path, once
		connected.  Larger than the connect timeout because this also
		covers the cloud thinking about a request, not just the link.
